{"type":"cards","design":"S_024","title":"Baseline challenges across United contact operations","lead":"Siloed care systems force agents into manual lookups across phone, chat, and Agent on Demand.","items":[{"title":"Multi-screen context switching","text":"Agents toggle between Cisco Webex, reservation records, and policy portals to piece together passenger context across 140 million annual journeys.","icon":"monitor-link"},{"title":"Manual policy and tariff lookups","text":"Locating exact fare rules, baggage policies, and irregular operations waivers requires keyword searches through static documents while the passenger waits.","icon":"report-search"},{"title":"Extended post-call documentation","text":"Writing interaction notes and selecting disposition codes after every voice or Agent on Demand session delays availability for the next queue.","icon":"file text"},{"title":"Inconsistent resolution paths","text":"Without real-time guided workflows on the desktop, complex rebooking and disruption inquiries depend heavily on individual agent tenure and recall.","icon":"workflow-path"}],"notes":"United Airlines supports over 140 million passengers annually across more than 300 global destinations through voice, chat, and Agent on Demand channels integrated with Cisco Webex. During high-volume travel windows, frontline agents lose valuable seconds switching between reservation screens, policy manuals, and wrap-up notes. Addressing these four baseline friction points is essential to lowering average handle time and preventing repeat calls.

