{"type":"cards","design":"S66_THREE_CARD_COLUMNS","title":"BigQuery partitioning and clustering strategy","items":[{"title":"Daily timestamp partitioning","icon":"database","points":["Column: daily partitioning configured on the raw vehicle event timestamp column","Filter rule: require_partition_filter set to true on all telemetry tables","Pruning: eliminates full-table scans when dispatch queries current shift activity"]},{"title":"Composite key clustering","icon":"network-nodes","points":["Order: clustered first by region identifier and second by vehicle ID","Block sorting: co-locates telemetry rows for the same truck in storage blocks","Maintenance: BigQuery automatically re-clusters incoming streaming blocks in the background"]},{"title":"Automated partition expiration","icon":"gear-dollar","points":["Lifecycle policy: partition_expiration_days configured on the hot operational telemetry table","Cost control: transitions aged partitions from active storage to long-term rates","Industry benchmark: partitioned streaming datasets achieve 8x to 16x higher cost efficiency"]}],"takeaway":"Daily partition pruning paired with vehicle ID and region clustering preserves low query latency while controlling compute spend.","notes":"We configure BigQuery tables with daily partitioning on the event timestamp and enforce partition filters so queries never scan historical data unnecessarily. Clustering by region and vehicle ID co-locates truck telemetry in storage blocks, while automated partition expiration controls active storage growth. Industry evaluations show that unified analytical architectures executing streaming ingestion directly on partitioned datasets achieve between 8x and 16x higher cost efficiency compared to disparate analytical engines.

